.TH HMM 1 2026-09-23 "hmm 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hmm \- run commands in drafts of the working directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hmm
.br
.B hmm run
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR path ]...
.RI [ command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B hmm ls
.RB [ \-a ]
.br
.B hmm diff
.RB [ \-\-stat ]
.RI [ draft ]
.br
.B hmm apply
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RI [ draft ]
.br
.B hmm merge
.RI [ draft ]
.br
.B hmm rm
.IR draft ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hmm
runs commands in drafts: copies of the working directory that a command
may change as it likes, while the working directory and the rest of the
system stay as they are.
It is meant for running coding agents, and anything else you would rather
not trust with your files.
.PP
A draft is a copy-on-write clone, so making one is quick and takes no
space until the command writes to it, and it includes everything in the
directory: the repository, uncommitted changes, and build output, so builds
start warm.
The command runs in the draft, which has the same name as the working
directory but lives under
.BR hmm 's
data directory (see
.BR FILES ),
and it is confined there: see
.BR SANDBOX .
.PP
When it makes a draft,
.B hmm
also records what the directory was, to compare the draft with later.
If the directory is the top of a git repository, that is the commit it is
at and the tree of its files, uncommitted changes included, which take
little time and no space in the repository; otherwise, it is a second
clone.
.PP
With no command,
.B hmm
lists the drafts of the working directory, as
.B hmm ls
does.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B hmm run \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIcommand \fR[\fIargument \fR...]]
Make a new draft of the working directory and run
.I command
in it, or your shell
.RB ( $SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
if it is not set) if no command is given.
Everything from
.I command
on belongs to the command, its own options included, so
.B hmm run claude \-\-resume
passes
.B \-\-resume
to
.BR claude .
When the command exits,
.B hmm
prints where the draft is, and exits with the command's exit status.
The draft stays until it is removed.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-n " name" "\fR, \fP\-\-name " name
Name the draft
.IR name ,
so that it can be referred to by the name rather than its ID.
A name is letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.B _
and
.BR . ,
not starting with
.BR \- ,
and is unique among the drafts of the working directory.
.TP
.BI \-w " path" "\fR, \fP\-\-write " path
Let the command write to
.I path
too, and to everything under it.
Can be repeated.
Commands that keep state in the home directory need it: for example,
.B hmm run \-w ~/.claude claude
lets Claude Code save its sessions and settings.
.RE
.TP
.B hmm ls \fR[\fB\-a\fR]
List the drafts of the working directory: their IDs, names, whether a
command is still running in them, how long ago they were made, and the
directory they are drafts of.
With
.BR \-a " (" \-\-all ),
list the drafts of every directory.
.TP
.B hmm diff \fR[\fB\-\-stat\fR] [\fIdraft\fR]
Show what changed in
.I draft
since it was made, or in the working directory's latest draft if none is
given, as a patch; with
.BR \-\-stat ,
only which files changed, and how much.
If the directory is a git repository, what it ignores, such as build
output, is left out.
.TP
.B hmm apply \fR[\fB\-\-check\fR] [\fIdraft\fR]
Make the changes that
.B hmm diff
shows in the directory the draft was made from: all of them or, if any
does not apply, because the directory has changed in the same places since
the draft was made, none.
Only files change: nothing is staged or committed.
A draft that is already applied is left alone, and so is one in which a
command is running.
With
.BR \-\-check ,
only say whether the changes apply.
The draft stays until it is removed.
.TP
.B hmm merge \fR[\fIdraft\fR]
Merge the commits made in
.IR draft ,
or in the working directory's latest draft if none is given, into the
branch the directory it was made from is on, as
.BR "git merge" (1)
does: a fast-forward if the branch has not moved since the draft was made,
a merge commit if it has, and, if the two changed the same lines, conflicts
to resolve and commit as for any merge.
Only commits are merged: changes the command did not commit are left in the
draft, and
.B hmm merge
says so.
The draft must have been made from the top of a git repository, and no
command may be running in it.
The commits stay in the repository as
.BI refs/hmm/ id
until the draft is removed.
.TP
.B hmm rm \fIdraft \fR...
Remove the drafts given, and everything in them, and the references to
their commits that
.B hmm merge
made.
A draft in which a command is running is not removed.
.PP
.B hmm \-\-help
prints a summary of the commands, and
.B hmm
.I command
.B \-\-help
of a command's options.
.SH DRAFTS
Every draft has an ID of six letters and digits, chosen at random when it is
made, such as
.BR k3xq9a ,
and a name if it was given one with
.BR \-n .
Wherever a draft is expected, it can be given by its name, if it is a draft
of the working directory, or by its ID, or by the start of its ID if no
other draft's ID starts the same way:
.B hmm rm k3
removes
.B k3xq9a
if it is the only draft whose ID starts with
.BR k3 .
Being random, IDs are not handed out again once removed, in practice.
.SH SANDBOX
The command runs under the macOS sandbox
.RB ( sandbox\-exec (1)),
with a profile that allows everything but:
.TP
Writing
Only the draft, the temporary directories
.RB ( /tmp
and
.BR $TMPDIR ),
devices, the caches that builds and package managers write to
.RB ( ~/.cargo ,
.BR ~/.rustup ,
.BR ~/.cache ,
.B ~/.npm
and
.BR ~/Library/Caches ),
and the paths given with
.B \-w
may be written to.
.TP
Reading
The contents of
.BR ~/.ssh ,
.BR ~/.aws ,
.B ~/.gnupg
and
.B ~/.config/gh
cannot be read, and neither can other drafts.
.PP
Everything else, including the network and the GPU, is allowed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/hmm/
The drafts, one directory each, named by ID.
In each:
.RS
.TP
.I tree/<name>/
The clone the command runs in and changes, named as the directory it was
made from.
.TP
.IR head ", " fork
If the directory is the top of a git repository: the commit it was at when
the draft was made, and the tree of its files then.
.TP
.I base/
Otherwise: a clone of the directory as it was when the draft was made.
.TP
.IR objects/ ", " fork.index ", " base.index ", " tree.index ", " git/
What
.B hmm
keeps to compare the draft with git: the objects it makes, kept out of the
directory's repository; its indexes of the directory and the draft; and, if
the directory is not a git repository, a repository of its own.
.TP
.I origin
The path of the directory it was made from.
While a command runs in the draft,
.B hmm
holds a lock on this file.
.TP
.I name
The draft's name, if it was given one.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hmm run
exits with the command's exit status, or 128 plus the number of the signal
that ended it.
The other commands exit with 0 if every draft given was found and dealt
with, and 1 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run Claude Code in a draft named
.BR parser :
.PP
.RS
.B hmm run \-n parser \-w ~/.claude claude
.RE
.PP
See the drafts of the working directory, what one changed, merge the
commits made in it, and remove it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B $ hmm
ID      NAME    STATE    AGE  ORIGIN
k3xq9a  parser  done      2h  ~/src/penberg/dwim
p7mw2d  \-       running   5m  ~/src/penberg/dwim
.B $ hmm diff parser
.B $ hmm merge parser
.B $ hmm rm parser
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
.B hmm
runs only on macOS for now, and needs the working directory to be on an
APFS volume, the same one as the home directory.
.PP
The command runs in the draft's path rather than the working directory's,
so tools that remember absolute paths, such as build caches, may rebuild.
.PP
The network is not confined: a command can send anything it can read to
anywhere, and can push to remotes whose credentials it can reach, such as
through the macOS keychain.
.PP
Commits made in a draft are not signed if signing needs
.B ~/.gnupg
or
.BR ~/.ssh ,
which the command cannot read; the merge commit
.B hmm merge
makes is.
.PP
A draft of a linked worktree
.RB ( git\-worktree (1))
shares its repository with the worktree, which the command cannot write
to, so commits cannot be made in it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-merge (1),
.BR sandbox\-exec (1),
.BR clonefile (2)
